$$\begin{align*}
\frac{2}{6-3\sqrt{3}}&=\frac{2(6+3\sqrt{3})}{(6-3\sqrt{3})(6+3\sqrt{3})}\\
\frac{2}{6-3\sqrt{3}}&=\frac{12+6\sqrt{3}}{36-27}\\
\frac{2}{6-3\sqrt{3}}&=\frac{4+2\sqrt{3}}{3}
\end{align*}$$
$$\begin{align*}
\frac{1}{2\sqrt{3}+3}&=\frac{2\sqrt{3}-3}{(2\sqrt{3}+3)(2\sqrt{3}-3)}\\
\frac{1}{2\sqrt{3}+3}&=\frac{2\sqrt{3}-3}{12-9}\\
\frac{1}{2\sqrt{3}+3}&=\frac{2\sqrt{3}-3}{3}
\end{align*}$$
$$\begin{align*}
\frac{2}{6-3\sqrt{3}}-\frac{1}{2\sqrt{3}+3}&=\frac{4+2\sqrt{3}-(2\sqrt{3}-3)}{3}\\
\frac{2}{6-3\sqrt{3}}-\frac{1}{2\sqrt{3}+3}&=\frac{7}{3}
\end{align*}$$
Since $7$ and $3$ are integers and $3\ne0$, the result is rational.
